  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.1,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Prior work (FAM:SF-CHT-064, FAM:SF-APPA-005, FAM:SF-APPM-021, FAM:SF38) indicates that measures of offensive change, whether in scheme or efficiency, suffer from low reliability and high noise, making it difficult to detect real effects or distinguish them from label error. This directly impacts the ability to reliably quantify 'how much the offense reorganizes' and 'change in points per possession'."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "The measurement of 'offensive reorganization' would likely rely on inferred labels from play-by-play data (e.g., changes in play-type distribution, player roles). Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPA-005, FAM:SF-APPM-021, FAM:SF38, FAM:SF-CHT-052) repeatedly shows that such inferred labels are prone to noise and error, making it difficult to accurately quantify reorganization."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
